const FAQ = [
  { q: 'Do I need Cloudflare, or any one vendor, to check signatures?', a: `No. The key directory sits on the agent's domain, and any site or vendor can check it by the draft's rules ${cite(1)}. Each verifier decides which agents it knows. Cloudflare's checks the directories registered with it ${cite(16)}.` },
  { q: 'Why not OAuth, or a JWT?', a: `Use them for what the agent may do. Web Bot Auth says which domain is asking and carries no scopes; an OAuth token or a JWT carries them ${cite(1, 32)}. ${a('#authz', 'Identity and authorization')} compares the two.` },
  { q: 'Does requiring signatures affect search?', a: `Googlebot doesn't sign, and Google tells sites to keep relying on IP addresses, reverse DNS and user agents alongside Web Bot Auth ${cite(5, 7)}. FORA's edge never refuses Googlebot, Bingbot or Applebot ${cite(30)}.` },
  { q: 'Will a signature rule refuse agents I want?', a: `It can. Google signs only some Google-Agent requests and tells sites to fall back to the older checks ${cite(5)}. On Arkose's network, most legitimate agent sessions carried no verified signature ${cite(18)}.` },
  { q: 'Does Web Bot Auth track my readers?', a: `No. It identifies agents, and a person reading your site signs nothing. One identifier links an agent's requests across sites, so the draft calls it unsuited to an identifier tied to one person ${cite(1)}.` },
  { q: 'Does my agent need to register anywhere for its signature to count?', a: `Not by the draft, which asks for no prior relationship ${cite(1)}. Cloudflare counts signatures from bots that registered their key directory through its bot submission form ${cite(16)}. DataDome asks operators to apply through a form ${cite(22)}. AWS WAF checks the signature against the agent's key directory and labels the result ${cite(20)}. A FORA Exchange checks a signature from any key directory that meets FORA's rules, and an account takes one signed request ${cite(25, 27)}.` },
];

